WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.091
Bigger model, more effort, or a chain?

2
00:00:02.371 --> 00:00:08.813
When output quality falls short, you have three main levers beyond rewording the prompt, and they cost different things.

3
00:00:09.093 --> 00:00:14.213
Raise effort first when the task needs more reasoning and you are already on a capable model.

4
00:00:14.493 --> 00:00:23.069
It is a one-line change, it keeps you on one model, and Claude’s effort docs recommend tuning it per workload and testing rather than assuming.

5
00:00:23.349 --> 00:00:32.309
Build a chain when the failure is specific and checkable, when different steps need different tools or context, or when you need a log of where things went wrong.

6
00:00:32.589 --> 00:00:39.245
Use a bigger model when errors persist across effort levels and the task needs capability the current model lacks.

7
00:00:39.525 --> 00:00:50.235
Anthropic’s prompt engineering overview makes the reverse point too: some problems, such as latency and cost, are better solved by choosing a different model than by more prompting.

8
00:00:50.515 --> 00:00:52.115
Notice the shared requirement.

9
00:00:52.395 --> 00:01:02.080
You can only choose between these with evidence, meaning the same test cases run under each option, compared on quality, cost per completed task, and latency.

10
00:01:02.360 --> 00:01:07.864
Without a test set, every lever looks like it helped, because you happened to try it on an input where it did.

11
00:01:08.144 --> 00:01:11.579
Lesson 6 builds that test set and the habit of using it.
